The tilde sigil
description: a deprecated back-edge marker on keys and entries; in value position, null as always
- >
  In value position `~` is unchanged - still null (`key: ~`). In **key position** yamlover once read a
  leading `~` as a back-edge sigil: `~key: *P` declared the reverse of the forward relation `key`, and
  `~- *P` a keyless reverse membership. Both are **deprecated in favor of path anchors** - `~key: *P`
  ≡ `&: P: key`, and `~- *P` ≡ `&: P[]` - still parsed through the migration window, serialized as
  anchors only.
- >
  In YAML, `~key:` is simply the plain-scalar key "~key" and `~-` a rare plain scalar - another place
  the same token reads differently, and the reason a literal `~` in a yamlover key is escaped:
  `\~key`. The sigil needs a **nonempty** name: a bare `~: value` line is not a back-edge but the
  **null-keyed entry** - the null key, adopted from YAML ([null keys](::language:vs-yaml:null-keys)).
